    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification \u2014 *\"a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their household members; with no existing household member no cap applies\"* \u2014 required two changes, since the concept of *household members* (plural) presupposes a household can hold more than one owner.\n\n**1. New capping step \u2014 `CapMembershipLevel`**\n- `src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/CapMembershipLevel.java`: after `AssignMembershipLevel` sets the raw level, this finds the maximum `membershipLevel` among existing (already-saved) owners sharing the new owner's deterministic `householdId`, and if the new owner's level exceeds `max + 1`, caps it to `max + 1`. With no existing household member (`maxExisting == null`), it leaves the level untouched. Mirrors the household-matching idiom of `AssignHouseholdSize`.\n- Wired into `owners.POST.yml` as `capMembershipLevel`, between `assignMembershipLevel` and `assignBulkSignupWarning`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\n**2. Relaxed `EnsureUniqueIdentity`**\n- Previously *any* second owner in the same household (same last name + postcode) was rejected 409, which made a multi-member household impossible. I narrowed the hard-duplicate rule to fire only for a *true* duplicate \u2014 an existing non-deleted owner in the same household **with the same E.164 telephone** (i.e. the same person). Distinct people in one household now coexist, while `DuplicateOwnerException` stays meaningful. This is faithful to the codebase's own stated notion (in `AssignPossibleDuplicate`) that \"same telephone is a hard duplicate concern.\"\n\nThe provided `AcceptanceTest.coreCapsLevelByHousehold` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\nOne thing worth flagging: the given spec sentence only described the cap. Relaxing the household-duplicate rule was a necessary consequence of the test requiring two same-household owners to both be created, but it does change the 409 duplicate behavior. I kept that change as narrow as I could (telephone-keyed rather than removing the rule); if you'd prefer a different duplicate policy, that's the piece to revisit.",
